Note: Although we have not fully resolved our problems in this area, it has 
become clear that other Alpha users are having similar problems.  I felt 
it was better to get out some useful (although incomplete) information, 
rather than wait to have *all* the answers.

Many thanks to all who responded to this question.

Original question/problem:

We are experiencing numerous problems with getting dial-up access to our 
system to work properly.  For test purposes, we are using the MS/Windows 
terminal software (emulating VT-100); however, other terminal emulation 
programs we have tried exhibit similar symptoms, which are:

   - system won't recognize CTRL-X
   - man pages don't scroll properly
   - other, similar incorrect responses

We are using a hunt&seek group of phone lines connected to five modems, 
which are connected in turn to a DecServer 90M. Our system is an Alpha AXPdu.txt 
300S running Digital Unix 3.2a.

If someone has a fix for this problem, or even a suggestion as to what we
should be looking at (server configuration, terminal configuration, etc.),
we would appreciate it. 

Summary of replies to date:

>From mstover@lis.pitt.eduMon May 15 08:25:42 1995
	There is a selection in the Settings:TerminalPreferences menu that
	tells windows whether to use control keys for windows commands or
	to pass them to the host. Deselect this option, this  works for me.

>From pobrien@draco.harvard.eduMon May 15 08:25:54 1995

Here are some suggestions: On the question of man pages not scrolling
correctly, type echo $TERM at the prompt and see if the system
believes you're using vt100.  Try stty rows 24 and see if that 
keeps the man pages from scrolling off the screen.  If the problem
of scrolling isn't that it scrolls off the page, but that it just
gives you a STDIN message and doesn't display the page, then it's 
again your terminal emulation.  

On the question of CTRL-X, the problem is likely your flow control.
You have flow control between the incoming modem and the terminal
server, and between the terminal server and the attached modem.
You have a choice in each case of XON/XOFF, RTS/DTS, and something
else, ( I can't think of it at the moment )  These choices are set
in the S registers of your modems.  If they understand the AT command
set, you would type AT&V to get the modem to display the numbers
associated with each S register.  You need the modem manual to 
tell you what each number represents.  You would use, for example,
to set register 5 to the value 2, the command AT S5=2.  

Basically you need to look at terminal emulation and flow control.

-Patrick O'Brien

>From ada@isz.chMon May 15 08:26:11 1995

hi kevin, i had similar problems, special with the manpage scrolling!
check the TERMINAL ID, sometimes it is DECVT-100, change that to VT-100.
in my environment, that change solves the scrolling problem!

